<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Harness for StreamPump's (WAITIDLE) dispatch barrier - unified streaming engine Step 3
    (docs/Architecture-Unified-Streaming-Engine.md). Drives the REAL StreamPump over the REAL
    TelnetStream against the REAL grblHAL simulator on loopback - no UI, no test server, no real
    hardware - and asserts the property the first (dwell-based) hardware test never exercised:
    the barrier must HOLD while the controller reports Run, and release only after two consecutive
    Idle reports, before the tail of the program is allowed onto the wire.

    Why a dwell couldn't test this: grblHAL acks G4 only after the dwell completes and reports Idle
    throughout, so the barrier armed against an already-idle controller and released trivially.
    Real G1 motion acks immediately (planner-buffered) and reports Run for the whole move - exactly
    the ack-vs-physical-motion gap WAITIDLE exists to close.

    The simulator binary and its EEPROM/littlefs state are copied from %AppData%\ioSender\Simulator
    to a temp work dir per run, so the user's own simulator state is never dirtied.

    Run with:  dotnet run (from tools/waitidle-probe)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>waitidle-probe</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\CNC Core\CNC Core\CNC.Core.net8.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
